<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacro Catino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sacro Catino is a green hexagonal dish kept in the treasury of San Lorenzo Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Genoese crusaders brought it back from the capture of Caesarea in 1101, believing it to be carved from a single emerald, and it was later identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>. It was broken when returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops had taken it there, and it proved to be made of glass.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacro-catino.jpg" alt="Sacro Catino" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relic_of_the_Last_Supper_-_Genoa_Cathedral_-_Genoa_2014.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The vessel is a hexagonal dish of bright green transparent material, now kept in the Museum of the Treasure of the Cathedral of San Lorenzo. It was presented as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>, or as the likeness of the dish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> used at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a>, but modern studies consider it an Islamic piece of the 9th or 10th century.</p><p>The belief that it was the Grail goes back to Jacopo da Varagine, whose Genoese Chronicle tells that soldiers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-embriaco">Guglielmo Embriaco</a> came into possession of it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. Writing in the second half of the 12th century, Archbishop William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> gave another account: the crusaders found the emerald dish in a temple built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> and bought it at a high price.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Early in the 14th century Cardinal Luca Fieschi held the dish as a pledge for a loan of 9,500 lire to the Compagna Comunis, which redeemed it in 1327 and ruled that it could never again be pledged or taken out of the cathedral's sacristy. Boucicault, the French governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, is said to have tried to steal it in 1409. When the army of Emperor Charles V sacked Genoa in 1522, the treasure was saved by the resistance of the priests and a payment of 1,000 ducats by the Republic of Genoa to the captain besieging the sacristy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1726 Gaetano di Santa Teresa gave its height as eight Genoese inches, about 16 cm, while the dish shown today is 9 cm high, which led some to suspect that a copy had been made to deter thieves. Taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1806, it was studied by the Académie des sciences of the Institut de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which found that it was not an emerald. It came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> on 14 June 1816 in ten pieces, one of them missing, and was restored in 1908, in 1951 and in 2017, the last time by the Opificio delle Pietre Dure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sacro Catino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Genoese fleets took part in the First Crusade, and Guglielmo Embriaco built siege engines at Jerusalem in 1099 and helped take Caesarea in 1101, bringing back booty that included the green glass bowl revered as the <b>Sacro Catino</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Baldwin I took Caesarea in May 1101 with a Genoese fleet, which received a share of the spoils including a green glass bowl believed to be the Holy Grail, now the <b>Sacro Catino</b> in Genoa.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Lordship of Caesarea</a></li></ul></section>
